{"meta": {"chapter_title": "Final Project and Professional Vision", "title": "Risk Mitigation and Family Communication", "description": "CAT 531 textbook section connecting risk mitigation and family communication to school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "course": "cat531-supplement", "chapter": "06", "section": "05", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."], "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_final_project_and_professional_vision", "cat531_supplement_risk_mitigation_and_family_communication", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "prereq_section_ids": ["cat531-supplement/06/04"], "practice_ids": ["cat531_supplement_6_5_risk_mitigation_and_family_communication_artifact_signal", "cat531_supplement_6_5_risk_mitigation_and_family_communication_annotation_signal", "cat531_supplement_6_5_risk_mitigation_and_family_communication_ai_boundary", "cat531_supplement_6_5_risk_mitigation_and_family_communication_calibration"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 5, "top_tier_hardened": false}, "content": "# Risk Mitigation and Family Communication\n\n![Risk Mitigation and Family Communication textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/06-05-risk-mitigation-and-family-communication.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nWhen a teacher adopts an AI tool, families inherit risks they did not choose: their child's writing may be stored, analyzed, or used to train a model; a reading-level label may follow the child across years; a free tool may monetize usage data. Risk mitigation is the work of identifying those risks before they materialize and reducing each to an acceptable level, and family communication is the obligation to disclose what remains in language families can actually act on. Done well, the two together convert a unilateral teacher decision into something closer to informed participation by the people it affects.\n\nThe artifact here is a school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks. A memo is not a reassurance (\"we use a FERPA-aware tool\"); it is a disclosure document that tells a specific reader exactly what data flows where, what the alternative is for families who decline, and how the school verified its own claims. The difference between reassurance and disclosure is the difference between public relations and informed consent.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA welcome letter announces: \"We are excited to use an innovative, FERPA-aware AI tool this year.\" It sounds responsible and discloses nothing. A parent reading it cannot tell what data leaves the building, whether their child's essays are stored, what \"FERPA-aware\" actually constrains, or whether they may decline. \"FERPA-aware\" is marketing language, not a data practice; many tools that store and analyze student work are technically FERPA-compliant while still doing exactly what a cautious family would want to know about.\n\nInformed consent requires that the family knows what they are consenting to. A letter that produces a comfortable feeling without conveying an actionable fact has not informed anyone; it has only managed their impression. The repair is to replace every reassurance with a specific, checkable disclosure.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA defensible risk-and-communication memo rests on four checks, each of which converts a vague worry into a concrete, verifiable practice:\n\n- **Privacy check.** What student data is collected, where is it stored, for how long, and is it used to train the vendor's model? \"Indefinite retention\" and \"30-day retention, no training use\" are different products, and a memo must state which one it actually is.\n- **Equity check.** Does the tool, or the opt-out path, disadvantage any group? An opt-out that routes the declining family through the same data-collecting upload is not a real alternative; a genuine opt-out offers a materially different path (teacher-only feedback, no upload).\n- **Consent check.** Is the disclosure something families can read and act on? Consent obtained from a notice written above a family's reading level, or only in a language they do not read, is not informed consent.\n- **Learning-purpose check.** Does the tool serve a stated instructional goal, so that the risk is taken in exchange for a real benefit rather than novelty?\n\nTwo disciplines make the checks trustworthy. First, **verify before you publish**: a retention figure or a privacy claim drafted by an AI assistant must be confirmed against the tool's actual data agreement, because a confidently stated wrong number is worse than an admitted unknown. Second, **make the opt-out genuinely different**, not a relabeled version of the same data flow. The course anchors apply directly: datafication drives the privacy check, critical edtech studies drives the equity check on who the default strands, and [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge) drives the learning-purpose check that justifies taking any risk at all.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A letter says: 'We use a trusted, FERPA-aware AI tool to keep your child secure.' A parent cannot tell what data leaves the building or whether they may decline. What has the letter done?\" explanation=\"It has reassured without disclosing. Warm, confident language lowers anxiety without transferring any actionable fact; FERPA-aware is marketing language, not a data practice, and many tools that store and analyze student work are technically compliant. A family that feels reassured has still not been informed.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Provided informed consent\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Managed the family impression without conveying an actionable fact\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Disclosed the retention period\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Offered a genuine opt-out\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nMatch each of the four checks to the precise question it forces the memo to answer:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Privacy check\",\"definition\":\"What data is collected, where is it stored, for how long, and is it used to train the vendor model?\"},{\"term\":\"Equity check\",\"definition\":\"Does the tool or its opt-out path disadvantage any group, and is the opt-out a materially different path?\"},{\"term\":\"Consent check\",\"definition\":\"Can families actually read and act on the disclosure, at the right reading level and in their languages?\"},{\"term\":\"Learning-purpose check\",\"definition\":\"Does the tool serve a stated instructional goal, so the risk buys a real benefit rather than novelty?\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nThe weak version below sounds plausible, but it does not yet give a reader enough evidence:\n\n> I made the school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks clearer.\n\nThat claim is too thin because \"clearer\" does not identify the audience, the problem, or the evidence. A stronger claim would read:\n\n> I revised the school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks so that the intended audience can see the decision, the constraint, and the evidence source. The revision is justified by **critical edtech studies**, and I can point to the exact part of the artifact that changed.\n\nThe improvement is not longer wording. It is a better trace. The reader can now see the artifact, the constraint from **critical edtech studies**, and the exact revision that followed.\n\n### Worked Example: An Annotated Family-Communication Decision\n\nFollow a teacher revising a family letter and the underlying AI-policy memo for a new AI tutoring tool.\n\n1. **Before (reassurance).** \"We are excited to use an innovative, FERPA-aware AI tool this year.\" A reviewer annotates: \"This reassures but discloses nothing a family can act on.\"\n2. **Disclose specifics.** Revision states: \"Your child's essays are sent to the AI tutor for feedback. Submissions are stored 30 days and are not used to train the model.\"\n3. **Make the opt-out real.** Draft opt-out routed every family through the same upload. Fix: opting out gives teacher-only feedback with no upload, a genuinely different path that avoids the declined risk.\n4. **Verify the claims.** The retention figure came from a BigAL draft. The teacher checks the tool's data agreement and corrects \"indefinite\" to the verified \"30 days\" before sending.\n5. **Make it accessible (after).** Reissue the letter at an 8th-grade reading level with a Spanish translation, because consent from a notice families cannot read is not informed consent.\n\nThe before reassures; the after discloses specific data handling, offers a real alternative, verifies the facts, and reaches every family.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Sending the AI-tutor letter home\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"You are about to send families a letter about a new AI tutoring tool that reads student essays. Your draft opens: We are excited to use an innovative, FERPA-aware AI tool this year. A parent will read this first. What do you fix first?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Nothing; FERPA-aware reassures families it is safe\",\"next\":\"reassure\",\"feedback\":\"FERPA-aware is marketing language, not a data practice. The letter reassures but discloses nothing a family can act on.\"},{\"label\":\"Replace the reassurance with what actually happens: essays are sent to the tutor, stored 30 days, not used to train the model\",\"next\":\"disclose\",\"feedback\":\"You led with the specific data practice before the benefit, which is what informed consent requires.\"},{\"label\":\"Add the word secure twice for emphasis\",\"next\":\"reassure\",\"feedback\":\"More reassurance is still not disclosure; no actionable fact has been transferred.\"}]},\"reassure\":{\"prompt\":\"A parent emails: what exactly happens to my child essays, and can I say no? Your letter cannot answer either question. What does this reveal?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"A comfortable feeling is not informed consent; I must disclose specifics\",\"next\":\"disclose\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Replace every reassurance with a checkable disclosure.\"}]},\"disclose\":{\"prompt\":\"You disclose the 30-day retention and no-training use. But that retention figure came from a BigAL draft, not the vendor agreement. Before sending, what must you do?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Send it; the AI sounded confident about 30 days\",\"next\":\"unverified\",\"feedback\":\"Shipping an unverified specific can turn a wrong 30 days into a promise the school cannot keep, which is worse than an admitted unknown.\"},{\"label\":\"Verify the figure against the tool actual data agreement first\",\"next\":\"optout\",\"feedback\":\"Verifying every concrete claim is the line between honest disclosure and an accidental lie.\"}]},\"unverified\":{\"prompt\":\"The real retention turns out to be indefinite. Families consented to a 30-day promise you cannot keep. What is the corrective rule?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Verify before you publish: confirm every specific against the agreement\",\"next\":\"optout\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly. A confidently stated wrong number is worse than an admitted unknown.\"}]},\"optout\":{\"prompt\":\"Your draft opt-out routes declining families through the same upload to the tutor. A parent who opts out still has their child essays sent. Is this a real opt-out?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Yes; they checked the opt-out box\",\"next\":\"cosmetic\",\"feedback\":\"A choice that leads to the same data collection is a cosmetic opt-out: the appearance of consent without its substance.\"},{\"label\":\"No; the opt-out must offer a materially different path, such as teacher-only feedback with no upload\",\"next\":\"access\",\"feedback\":\"Right. A genuine opt-out avoids the declined risk.\"}]},\"cosmetic\":{\"prompt\":\"Trace what actually happens to a child whose family declines. It is identical to the default. What must change?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Make the opt-out a different path: teacher-only feedback, no upload\",\"next\":\"access\",\"feedback\":\"Correct, a real alternative is the substance of consent.\"}]},\"access\":{\"prompt\":\"The letter now discloses verified specifics and a real opt-out. But it is written above many families reading level and only in English, though a third of your families read Spanish. What remains?\",\"feedback\":\"Consent obtained from a notice families cannot read is not informed consent, and since the tool benefits English learners most, the access gap is also an equity problem: the families who would benefit most cannot meaningfully consent. Reissue the letter at an 8th-grade reading level with a Spanish translation. Only then does the memo inform every family rather than soothe the ones who needed it least.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nDisclose the specific data practice before you describe the benefit. A family should be able to learn, from the first paragraph, exactly what happens to their child's data and what their alternative is; the excitement about the tool belongs after the facts they need to make a choice.\n\nRun the memo through three checks before it goes home:\n\n1. **Disclosure check:** Does it state what data is collected, where it goes, how long it is kept, and whether it trains a model?\n2. **Alternative check:** Is the opt-out a materially different path, and have I verified every factual claim against the tool's actual agreement?\n3. **Access check:** Can every family read it, at the right reading level and in the languages they speak?\n\nIf any answer is missing, the memo manages impressions rather than enabling informed consent.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"An AI assistant drafts a sentence for your family memo: 'Your child data is never shared with any third party and is deleted after 30 days.' Explain why this fluent, confident sentence is more dangerous than writing 'we are confirming the retention terms,' and state the rule it violates.\" answer=\"The sentence is dangerous precisely because it is fluent and specific: an AI will happily generate a plausible retention period and a confident no-sharing claim, and if you ship it unchecked, a wrong 30 days or a false no third-party sharing becomes a promise the school cannot keep, an accidental lie families relied on. An admitted unknown is honest; a confidently stated wrong fact is not. It violates the verify-before-you-publish rule: every concrete claim must be confirmed against the tool actual data agreement before it goes home, because verification is the line between disclosure and deception, not optional polish.\"></self-explain>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_risk_mitigation_and_family_communication\",\"cat531_supplement_final_project_and_professional_vision\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe classic error is mistaking reassurance for disclosure. Warm, confident language (\"trusted,\" \"secure,\" \"FERPA-aware\") lowers anxiety without transferring any actionable fact, and a family that feels reassured has still not been informed. A second error is the cosmetic opt-out: offering families a choice that leads to the same data collection, which provides the appearance of consent without its substance.\n\nThe most damaging misreading is publishing unverified specifics. An AI assistant will happily generate a plausible retention period or compliance claim; if the teacher ships it without checking the data agreement, a wrong \"30 days\" or false \"no third-party sharing\" becomes a promise the school cannot keep. Verifying every concrete claim is not optional polish; it is the line between honest disclosure and an accidental lie.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nA school is rolling out an AI tool that records and transcribes class discussions for English learners. Begin with the privacy check: the tool captures children's voices, so the memo must state where recordings go, that retention is 14 days, and that audio is not used for training, each claim confirmed against the vendor contract. Then the equity check: the tool helps English learners most, but if the only consent form is in English, the families who would benefit most cannot meaningfully consent, so the access problem is also an equity problem.\n\nNow build the real alternative and the purpose justification. The opt-out cannot simply mute the child; it must offer an equivalent support (a peer note-taker, a teacher transcript) so declining does not cost the student the benefit. The learning-purpose check states why the recording is worth any residual risk: it lets the teacher give targeted oral-language feedback that handwriting samples cannot reveal. The finished memo lets a family in any of the school's languages understand what is captured, decline without penalty, and trust that every stated fact was verified, which is what mitigation and communication look like when they are done as professional obligations rather than announcements.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read a family memo, mark every sentence as either a disclosure (a checkable fact about data, alternatives, or purpose) or a reassurance (a feeling-management phrase). A trustworthy memo is mostly disclosures; a weak one is mostly reassurances. Then test the opt-out: trace what actually happens to a child whose family declines, and confirm it differs from the default. By the end of CAT 531 06.05 you should be able to read any AI rollout notice and tell within a minute whether it informs families or merely soothes them.\n\nA strong reader of a risk-and-communication memo can answer four questions:\n\n1. What does the memo actually tell families about data collection, storage, and training use?\n2. Is the opt-out a genuinely different path, or the same data flow relabeled?\n3. Was every concrete claim verified against the tool's real agreement?\n4. Can every family, across reading levels and languages, read and act on it?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA memo is strong when a family that declines knows exactly what changes for their child, and when every factual claim could be confirmed against the vendor agreement on demand. Friendly tone is the last layer; the load-bearing layers are verified disclosure, a real alternative, and genuine accessibility. If a claim is unverified, mark it and ask BigAL to compare a reassurance sentence with a disclosure sentence rather than to \"make the letter warmer.\"\n\nA useful closing line would read: \"I replaced 'FERPA-aware' with the verified retention period and a no-upload opt-out because a family cannot consent to a risk the letter never named, and the equity decision in my plan fails if the only families who can read the notice are the ones who needed it least.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the memo from a single AI tutor to a district-wide one-to-one device program where dozens of apps collect data. The four checks still govern, but the disclosure problem scales: families now need a clear summary of which categories of data the program collects and a single, navigable opt-out rather than one notice per app. Verification becomes a recurring obligation as vendors change terms. If you understood communication as verified, actionable disclosure, you design a consolidated, maintainable notice. If you only memorized the single-tool letter, families will drown in twenty unread reassurances and consent to none of them in any meaningful sense.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/06-05-risk-mitigation-and-family-communication-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"06.05\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the school AI policy memo with privacy, equity, consent, and learning-purpose checks for CAT 531 06.05. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_risk_mitigation_and_family_communication\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace records evidence source, risk, support choice, and revision quality. That is what separates professional judgment from simple completion.\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat531_supplement_6_5_risk_mitigation_and_family_communication_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_risk_mitigation_and_family_communication", "stem": "A teacher drafts a family letter about a new AI tutoring tool. Which version best mitigates risk and respects families' right to decide?", "options": ["\"We are excited to use an innovative AI tool this year to boost learning.\"", "\"Your child's writing will be sent to an AI tutor for feedback. The tool stores submissions for 30 days and does not use them for training. You may opt your child into teacher-only feedback by replying by Friday.\"", "\"We use industry-standard, FERPA-aware technology to support instruction.\"", "\"The district has approved several tools; details are available on request.\""], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Risk-mitigating family communication is specific about what data is shared, how long it is kept, and how to opt out, in plain language.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_6_5_risk_mitigation_and_family_communication_vague_reassurance", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_final_project_and_professional_vision", "cat531_supplement_risk_mitigation_and_family_communication", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_6_5_risk_mitigation_and_family_communication_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_risk_mitigation_and_family_communication", "stem": "A reviewer annotates a policy memo: \"You offer an opt-out, but every alternative you list still requires the same login and uploads the same data. The opt-out is not real.\" What does this expose?", "options": ["A grammar problem in the opt-out sentence.", "A consent defect: a meaningful opt-out must provide a genuinely different option that avoids the risk being declined.", "The memo is simply too short.", "A missing logo on the letterhead."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Consent is only meaningful when declining leads to a real alternative; a fake opt-out is a risk, not a mitigation.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_6_5_risk_mitigation_and_family_communication_hollow_optout", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_risk_mitigation_and_family_communication", "evidence_based_revision"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_6_5_risk_mitigation_and_family_communication_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning", "stem": "A teacher asks BigAL to draft the family AI-policy memo. What is the defensible boundary on using the draft?", "options": ["Send the model's draft as written, since it covers privacy and equity by default.", "Use the draft for structure, then verify each factual claim (retention period, training use, opt-out path) against the tool's actual data agreement before sending.", "Ask the model to assure families the tool is safe so the letter is reassuring.", "Let the model decide which families need the consent form."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A model can scaffold the memo, but the teacher must verify every data claim against the real agreement, because families rely on its accuracy.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_6_5_risk_mitigation_and_family_communication_unverified_claims", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_6_5_risk_mitigation_and_family_communication_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_risk_mitigation_and_family_communication", "stem": "A policy memo is written at a college reading level and only in English, in a school where many families read at a different level or speak other languages. Why does this weaken the consent it seeks, even if every fact is correct?", "options": ["It will take longer for families to read.", "Consent obtained from a notice families cannot fully understand is not informed consent, so the equity and consent checks fail.", "The memo should always be exactly one page.", "Reading level never affects the validity of a consent form."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Informed consent requires comprehensible communication; accessibility of the notice is part of the equity and consent checks, not a formatting nicety.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_6_5_risk_mitigation_and_family_communication_accessibility_optional", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_risk_mitigation_and_family_communication", "evidence_based_revision"]}]}, "content_version": {"content_version": "353a4fb658a13cd9775c5cfcdf91a36c900f78630eca054e84987db67c6585f1", "algorithm": "sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1", "parts": ["mdx", "meta", "practice", "misconceptions"]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "cat531_supplement_6_5_risk_mitigation_and_family_communication_vague_reassurance", "pattern": "reassurance_instead_of_disclosure", "description": "Communicating excitement or vague reassurance instead of specific data, retention, and opt-out facts", "trigger": "family letter says innovative or industry-standard but never states what data is shared or how to decline", "feedback": "State exactly what data is shared, how long it is kept, and how families can opt out, in plain language.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_6_5_risk_mitigation_and_family_communication_hollow_optout", "pattern": "opt_out_without_alternative", "description": "Offering an opt-out whose alternatives still carry the same risk being declined", "trigger": "every listed alternative uses the same login and uploads the same data", "feedback": "Provide a genuinely different option that avoids the declined risk, or the opt-out is not real consent.", "rail_action": "ask"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_6_5_risk_mitigation_and_family_communication_unverified_claims", "pattern": "unverified_data_claims", "description": "Sending AI-drafted privacy claims to families without checking them against the tool's actual agreement", "trigger": "retention or training-use statements are taken from the model draft and not verified", "feedback": "Verify every data claim against the real data agreement before the memo reaches families.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_6_5_risk_mitigation_and_family_communication_accessibility_optional", "pattern": "accessibility_treated_as_optional", "description": "Treating reading level and translation as formatting rather than as part of informed consent", "trigger": "memo is English-only at a high reading level in a mult